Feed macOS 27 direct touches into GPUI through a gesture recognizer on GPUIView.

What: a new `gpui_macos` module, `window/touch_recognizer.rs`: `GPUITouchRecognizer`, an `NSGestureRecognizer`
subclass overriding `touches{Began,Moved,Ended,Cancelled}WithEvent:` and `reset` (Apple's custom-recognizer pattern,
`NSGestureRecognizer (NSSubclassUse)`), added to every GPUIView when `isOperatingSystemAtLeastVersion(27.0)`, with
`allowedTouchTypes` Direct. Each callback (one handler per `NSTouchPhase`, `handle_touches::<PHASE>`) reads the
event's changed touches (`touchesMatchingPhase:inView:` on the view) and turns those in its own phase into
`TouchEvent`s through 0025's `TouchIds::track_callback` (position from `locationInView:`, y flipped as
for mouse events; device `Touch`; `time_stamp` from `NSEvent.timestamp` by 0024's `uptime_instant`), hands them to
GPUI through 0025's `TouchQueue`, and sets its state by 0025's `next_state`. Its `reset` calls super, then cancels
every touch still live (where it last was) and forgets them. `window.rs`: the class is registered in
`build_classes`, `MacWindowState` holds the window's `TouchIds` and `TouchQueue`, the recognizer is added where the
view is made; `handle_view_event` drops 0025's `is_touch_copy` events while the recognizer holds touches, and
GPUIView handles `mouseCancelled:` (macOS 26) as a mouse up (`events.rs`: `NSEventTypeMouseCancelled`, 40, newer
than objc2-app-kit, joins the mouse-up arm), so no button stays down when AppKit takes a press over. GPUI's own
recognizers then make taps, pans, long presses and pinches of the touches, as on iOS.

Why: AppKit hands direct touches to gesture recognizers only, never to a view's responder methods (Apple's macOS 27
notes), and GPUI's window and view classes are private to gpui_macos, so neither a Parallax crate nor an extension
trait can add one. cfg: the crate's own (macOS); before macOS 27 no recognizer is added and nothing changes.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

